<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="aa0b7b84">&lt;p&gt;
    Using knowledge of the project approach and scope, the resources required to execute the project within the desired
    timeline and costs should be identified. The focus is on stakeholder personnel who are direct participants in the
    project. In some cases, third party personnel or sub-contractors may also be involved.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    After identifying the involved parties, their respective roles and responsibilities for governance and project delivery
    are allocated to the activity definitions and work estimates. This process considers the competencies needed to perform
    the work and the organizational accountability for various aspects of service delivery. For example, only specific
    individuals may approve final work products, but a mixed team of resources may be involved in the production of the
    work product. Consider:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Who will need to be involved (decision authority, competencies, roles)?
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        What level of involvement (full-time, part-time) is expected?
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        How does the timeline affect the number of resources needed?
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        What will the resources cost?
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    This process may be iterative as there could be timeline or financials constraints that affect the staffing, or
    vice-versa. Beyond the direct work responsibilities, the project governance structure or functional organization is
    also considered. Common roles include executive project sponsor, PM, project team lead, subject matter resource, team
    member, steering committee member, etc. As the team is defined, roles such as these should be documented in a resource
    plan.&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
